repl_step — evaluation
=======================

status: planned (2026-07-09)
focal tool: repl_step (plans/repl_step)

task
----
the hot path: drive a multi-step proof, including surviving failed
attempts. the single most important behavioral claim to check is the
description's warning — a failed step leaves state UNCHANGED, do NOT
call repl_back after a failure.

setup
-----
fresh server. the lemma needs induction + a non-obvious step so that
at least one failed attempt is near-certain (that failure is the
point of the evaluation, not a defect of it).

prompt
------
"Prove `lemma \"foldr (+) xs (0::nat) = sum_list xs\"` in
Isabelle/HOL, working step by step so you can inspect goals as you
go. Give me the final Isar text."

success
-------
proof completed; after every failed step the agent's next action was
consistent with unchanged state (retry a different step — NOT an
undo).

signals
-------
- THE trap: after a failed `by simp`, does the agent call repl_back
  (discarding a good step) because its editor intuition says "undo
  the failure"? the description warns against exactly this — the
  evaluation measures whether the warning is read and believed.
  count occurrences across runs; this number decides whether the
  warning must escalate (into the error text of the failed step
  itself, the strongest placement).
- step granularity: one Isar command per call, or does it paste
  multi-command blobs? (the plan pins one-command semantics; blobs
  produce confusing errors.)
- failure loops: same failing step retried verbatim more than once?
  (recovery axis — the error text may not be informative enough.)

reflection
----------
- if the back-after-failure trap fires even once per run set, put
  the state-unchanged notice INSIDE the failed-step error reply, not
  only in the description (agents demonstrably read errors more
  than descriptions).
- if goal output after each step is too verbose/too terse for the
  agent to plan the next step, output-format spec question.
